WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.311
Agent monitoring should look for behavior
that represents meaningful risk.

2
00:00:07.391 --> 00:00:12.839
Examples include repeated policy denials,
unusual tool use,

3
00:00:12.919 --> 00:00:19.625
unexpected data access, high-volume
retrieval, abnormal failed actions,

4
00:00:19.705 --> 00:00:26.097
attempts to access unauthorized resources,
unexpected memory writes,

5
00:00:26.177 --> 00:00:32.359
unusual prompt patterns at a high level,
and tool argument anomalies.

6
00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:36.735
The point is not to alert on every strange
phrase.

7
00:00:36.815 --> 00:00:43.102
The point is to detect patterns that could
indicate misuse, confusion,

8
00:00:43.182 --> 00:00:46.116
compromise, or control failure.

9
00:00:46.196 --> 00:00:51.016
Alert severity should reflect impact and
confidence.

10
00:00:51.096 --> 00:00:55.916
A single denied low-risk lookup may be
informational.

11
00:00:55.996 --> 00:01:03.226
Repeated attempts to access restricted
data across several sessions may deserve

12
00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:04.773
investigation.

13
00:01:04.853 --> 00:01:11.454
A write-capable tool call outside normal
patterns may need urgent review.

14
00:01:11.534 --> 00:01:17.192
Detections should combine event type, user
context, tool risk,

15
00:01:17.272 --> 00:01:21.882
data sensitivity, policy outcome, volume,
timing,

16
00:01:21.962 --> 00:01:25.734
and historical baseline where available.

17
00:01:25.814 --> 00:01:30.215
False positives and false negatives both
matter.

18
00:01:30.295 --> 00:01:37.525
A false positive is an alert that reports
a problem that did not occur as described.

19
00:01:37.605 --> 00:01:44.416
Too many false positives create alert
fatigue and train teams to ignore agent

20
00:01:44.496 --> 00:01:45.649
monitoring.

21
00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:52.435
A false negative is a real problem or
risky behavior that monitoring fails to

22
00:01:52.515 --> 00:01:53.248
detect.

23
00:01:53.328 --> 00:01:59.824
Tuning should improve signal quality over
time, using incident lessons,

24
00:01:59.904 --> 00:02:03.781
analyst feedback, and changing workflows.

25
00:02:03.861 --> 00:02:06.900
Good detections are explainable.

26
00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:13.791
An analyst should understand why the alert
fired, which events contributed,

27
00:02:13.871 --> 00:02:20.053
what evidence is available, and what
response steps are recommended.

28
00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:27.048
Agent detections should connect to
response workflows, not just dashboards.

29
00:02:27.128 --> 00:02:33.625
The best monitoring programs treat alerts
as hypotheses to investigate,

30
00:02:33.705 --> 00:02:38.420
backed by traceable events and clear
severity logic.
